    <p class="leading-relaxed text-foreground/90 mb-6">Nesso is the only one of the five that combines the iMessage extension, AI drafting, and a free tier in a single app. That's not marketing — it's the actual reason it was built.</p>

    <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-foreground mt-8 mb-3">Why we built Nesso</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-foreground/90 mb-6">I'm Brady. I started Nesso at 17 because I lost touch with most of my high school friends within a year of them leaving for college. The existing personal-CRM apps were either built for sales reps or hidden behind a $12 a month paywall. I just wanted to remember to text people. Nesso is free because friendship maintenance shouldn't cost $144 a year.</p>

    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold text-foreground tracking-[-0.02em] mt-14 mb-3">How to keep long-distance friendships alive</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-foreground/90 mb-6">Long-distance friendships die for a different reason than local ones. You don't bump into the person, so the friendship has to be 100% scheduled. The fix is the opposite of intuitive: don't try to schedule big things. Schedule small things.</p>

    <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-foreground mt-8 mb-3">The anchor ritual</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-foreground/90 mb-6">Pick one recurring micro-thing. A weekly meme drop. A Sunday-night voice note. A monthly "what are you watching" check-in. The anchor is the floor, not the ceiling — you can always do more, but the anchor never breaks. Long-distance friendships that survive 5+ years almost always have one.</p>

    <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-foreground mt-8 mb-3">Why an iMessage extension matters more than you'd think</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-foreground/90 mb-6">For iPhone-to-iPhone friendships, the iMessage extension is the difference between "I'll send something later" and actually sending it. Switching out of Messages to a CRM app to draft a check-in is friction. Friction kills consistency. Consistency is the entire game.</p>

    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold text-foreground tracking-[-0.02em] mt-14 mb-3">How to stay in touch with family without it feeling like a chore</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-foreground/90 mb-6">Family relationships have different rules than friendships. Cadence is more obligatory but contact mode matters more.</p>

    <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-foreground mt-8 mb-3">Parents and grandparents — cadence plus phone, not text</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-foreground/90 mb-6">For parents and grandparents, voice beats text. A 4-minute phone call once a week registers more deeply than 14 texts. For grandparents specifically, a phone call is often the only mode of contact they fully experience as connection. Set a recurring time — Sunday afternoons are a classic — and treat it as a non-negotiable appointment with yourself.</p>

    <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-foreground mt-8 mb-3">Siblings — group chat hygiene</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-foreground/90 mb-6">Siblings die in the chaos of family group chats. The trick: have a separate chat with each sibling, in addition to the family one. The 1:1 chat is where actual updates happen. The family group is for logistics and memes. Mixing them means everyone defaults to surface-level.</p>

    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold text-foreground tracking-[-0.02em] mt-14 mb-3">Streaks, badges, and the psychology of showing up</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-foreground/90 mb-6">Snapchat figured out something most relationship apps don't: people respond to consistency rituals. They also got it half-wrong by making the streak the <em>point</em>.</p>

    <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-foreground mt-8 mb-3">Why Snapchat got streaks half-right</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-foreground/90 mb-6">Snapchat streaks created millions of friendships that wouldn't have survived otherwise. They also created friendships where the only content of the relationship was the streak. The ritual became hollow — two people sending blank photos to a contact they hadn't actually talked to in months.</p>

    <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-foreground mt-8 mb-3">How gentle pressure beats guilt</h3>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-foreground/90 mb-6">The fix is streaks tied to <em>actual reach-outs</em>, not to opening an app. If the streak counts every meaningful check-in (any kind: text, call, voice note, in-person hangout), it stays meaningful. If it counts opening an app, it becomes performative. The pressure should always point toward the friendship, not toward the tool.</p>

